{"command": "echo \"=== spec.md ===\" && cat /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md && echo \"\" && echo \"=== requirements.md ===\" && cat /testbed/specs/001-transactions/checklists/requirements.md && echo \"\" && echo \"=== feature.json ===\" && cat /testbed/.specify/feature.json", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "=== spec.md ===\n# Feature Specification: Arithmetic Utility\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-arithmetic-utility`\n\n**Created**: 2026-09-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Unscored arithmetic utility, unrelated to the scored TinyDB features. R01: create /testbed/smoke.py with add(a,b) returning a+b. R02: support integers and floats without changing TinyDB source. R03: add and run tests, including negative values and zero. Build the small utility through every requested workflow phase.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing\n\n### User Story 1 - Basic Addition (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer needs a simple, standalone Python utility that performs addition of two numeric values. The utility should be immediately usable without any configuration or dependencies beyond the Python standard library.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core functionality - without a working add function, the utility has no value.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by importing the module and calling `add(a, b)` with known values, verifying the result matches `a + b`.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(2, 3)` is called, **Then** the result is `5`\n2. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(0, 0)` is called, **Then** the result is `0`\n3. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(-1, 1)` is called, **Then** the result is `0`\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Float Support (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer needs the utility to correctly handle floating-point numbers in addition operations, ensuring numerical accuracy for decimal values.\n\n**Why this priority**: Float support is a natural extension of basic addition and is required for real-world use cases involving measurements, financial calculations, or scientific data.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by calling `add` with float arguments and verifying the result matches expected float arithmetic.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(1.5, 2.5)` is called, **Then** the result is `4.0`\n2. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(-0.5, 0.5)` is called, **Then** the result is `0.0`\n3. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(0.1, 0.2)` is called, **Then** the result is `0.3`\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Zero and Negative Value Handling (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer needs the utility to correctly handle edge cases involving zero and negative numbers, ensuring robustness across the full range of numeric inputs.\n\n**Why this priority**: Edge case handling ensures the utility is reliable in production. While not the primary use case, these scenarios are common in real-world applications.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by calling `add` with combinations of zero and negative numbers and verifying correct results.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(-5, -3)` is called, **Then** the result is `-8`\n2. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(10, 0)` is called, **Then** the result is `10`\n3. **Given** the `smoke.py` module exists with an `add` function, **When** `add(0, -7)` is called, **Then** the result is `-7`\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- What happens when `add()` is called with non-numeric types (e.g., strings, None)? The utility should raise a `TypeError`.\n- How does the system handle very large numbers (Python handles arbitrary precision integers natively)?\n- How does the system handle mixed integer and float inputs?\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001**: The system MUST provide a module at `/testbed/smoke.py` containing a function `add(a, b)` that returns the sum of its two arguments.\n- **FR-002**: The `add(a, b)` function MUST support integer inputs and return their sum.\n- **FR-003**: The `add(a, b)` function MUST support float inputs and return their sum.\n- **FR-004**: The `add(a, b)` function MUST handle zero values correctly (e.g., `add(0, 5)` returns `5`).\n- **FR-005**: The `add(a, b)` function MUST handle negative values correctly (e.g., `add(-3, -4)` returns `-7`).\n- **FR-006**: The `add(a, b)` function MUST handle mixed integer and float inputs correctly.\n- **FR-007**: The utility MUST be completely independent of TinyDB source code and must not modify any TinyDB files.\n- **FR-008**: The system MUST include a test suite that verifies all functional requirements.\n\n### Key Entities\n\n- **smoke.py**: A standalone Python module providing the `add(a, b)` function. Contains no external dependencies beyond Python standard library.\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: All test cases pass including positive integers, negative integers, zero, floats, and mixed types.\n- **SC-002**: The `add(a, b)` function correctly computes the sum for all tested input combinations with 100% accuracy.\n- **SC-003**: The utility module contains no imports beyond the Python standard library.\n- **SC-004**: No TinyDB source files are modified or referenced by the utility.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The utility is a standalone script/module, not integrated into TinyDB's codebase.\n- Python 3.10+ is available (consistent with TinyDB's target).\n- The utility does not require any external dependencies.\n- Test execution uses the project's existing test infrastructure (pytest).\n- The `/testbed/specs/001-transactions` directory is the designated workflow artifacts location.\n- Type coercion between int and float is handled by Python's native arithmetic (no explicit conversion required).\n\n=== requirements.md ===\n# Specification Quality Checklist: Arithmetic Utility\n\n**Purpose**: Validate specification completeness and quality before proceeding to planning\n**Created**: 2026-09-17\n**Feature**: [Link to spec.md](../spec.md)\n\n## Content Quality\n\n- [x] No implementation details (languages, frameworks, APIs)\n- [x] Focused on user value and business needs\n- [x] Written for non-technical stakeholders\n- [x] All mandatory sections completed\n\n## Requirement Completeness\n\n- [x] No [NEEDS CLARIFICATION] markers remain\n- [x] Requirements are testable and unambiguous\n- [x] Success criteria are measurable\n- [x] Success criteria are technology-agnostic (no implementation details)\n- [x] All acceptance scenarios are defined\n- [x] Edge cases are identified\n- [x] Scope is clearly bounded\n- [x] Dependencies and assumptions identified\n\n## Feature Readiness\n\n- [x] All functional requirements have clear acceptance criteria\n- [x] User scenarios cover primary flows\n- [x] Feature meets measurable outcomes defined in Success Criteria\n- [x] No implementation details leak into specification\n\n## Notes\n\n- All items passed validation on first review.\n- The specification is ready for `$speckit-clarify` or `$speckit-plan`.\n\n=== feature.json ===\n{\n  \"feature_directory\": \"specs/001-transactions\"\n}\n"}